nt keep Bottoms competitive", "Six of seven raters call it a Tossup; markets split between the parties"], "rationale": "Every recent poll and all but one expert rating put this race within a point or two of even, with Jackson showing a narrow but consistent edge in mid-October surveys and holding a roughly 20-to-1 fundraising advantage. The Democratic-favorable environment, the absence of Kemp from the ballot, and heavy Republican-leaning sponsorship of the polling keep the race effectively a coin flip that tilts marginally to Jackson.", "watch": "A turnout surge among metro Atlanta Democrats or voter backlash to Jackson skipping debates could erase his thin edge, as could a late advertising blitz funded by his war chest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's governorship is open for the first time in years with Brian Kemp term-limited, and the contest between Republican Rick Jackson and Democrat Keisha Lance Bottoms is among the closest in the country. Mid-October polling shows Jackson ahead by one to two points, though most surveys come from Republican-leaning pollsters, and published averages cluster around a two-point Republican margin. Six of seven professional raters call the race a tossup; the Silver Bulletin alone leans Democratic. Betting markets split, pricing Democratic chances between roughly 46 and 51 percent.\n\nThe fundamentals cut in both directions. The national environment favors Democrats and the state's partisan lean is nearly even, but Jackson holds an enormous financial advantage, having raised about $119 million to Bottoms's $6 million. Jackson's debate absences have drawn attention without clearly moving the numbers. With early voting underway, this remains a coin flip with a slight Republican tilt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6414, "margin": 1.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5561, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5612, "margi